      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the value of \\(\\sin \\frac{11 \\pi}{3}\\), we need to first understand that the trigonometric functions are periodic with a period of \\(2\\pi\\). Therefore, we can find the sine of \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) by finding an equivalent angle between 0 and \\(2\\pi\\).\n\nFirst, we reduce \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) modulo \\(2\\pi\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\n\\]\nSince \\(3\\pi = 2\\pi + \\pi\\), we have:\n\\[\n3\\pi + \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = 2\\pi + \\pi + \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = 2\\pi + \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\n\\]\nThus, \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is coterminal with \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\), which means \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3} = \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nThe angle \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) is in the fourth quadrant. The reference angle for \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\frac{5\\pi}{3} - \\pi = \\frac{5\\pi}{3} - \\frac{3\\pi}{3} = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\n\\]\nIn the fourth quadrant, the sine of an angle is negative:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = -\\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\n\\]\nWe know that \\(\\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
